.TH SCH 5
.SH NAME
sch \- schematic source
.SH DESCRIPTION
A schematic source describes the pages of a schematic the way they
would be said: short statements, each placing one thing against
something else.
It has no coordinates and no designators; the programs compute the
geometry, and the designators come from the page number.
One statement per line, words separated by blanks; # starts a comment.
The first word says what the statement is.
Within a page the statements may come in any order: names are looked
up once all are read, and the parts are placed in the order their
anchors allow.
.PP
.RS
.nf
page NAME
title TEXT
field NAME TEXT
sheet TEMPLATE
scale S
group NAME
end
port NAME PIN
note TEXT
KIND NAME [VALUE] [rating R] [mpn M] [side WAY] [flip] [PLACE]
wire PIN to PIN [WAY]
ground at PIN
chassis at PIN
supply NAME at PIN
global NAME at PIN
nc PIN
.fi
.RE
.PP
PLACE is one of
.PP
.RS
.nf
WAY from PIN            joined to the pin
WAY of PART             next to the part, not joined
beside PART [WAY]       in parallel with the part
between PIN and PIN WAY joined to both pins, out the way past them
.fi
.RE
.PP
and a WAY is
.BR left ,
.BR right ,
.B up
or
.BR down .
.SS Pages
.B page
opens a page, and the page holds every statement up to the next
.B page
or the end of the file; a file has one page or more, and a statement
before the first is a fault.
The pages are numbered by their order in the file, from 1 (99 at
most): the designators of the fourth page run R401, R402, ....
NAME, a name and no number, once in the file, is the top group of the
page: its parts are named NAME.group...part,
as outputs.ch1.gain.rf.
.B title
and
.B field
fill the title block of the page (Title, Rev, Date, Drawn; File and
Page are filled in); the title is NAME unless said.
.B sheet
names the template, sch_a by default;
.B scale
enlarges the drawing, 1 by default.
.SS Groups
.B group
opens a group within the one open and
.B end
closes it.
A group is a block: its parts are named within it, and the netlist
names them by their path, as outputs.ch1.gain.rf.
A name is found in the group open, then in each group round it, out to
the page.
.B port
names a pin as part of the interface of the group: round it, gain.out
stands for that pin.
A port outside any group is a port of the sheet, drawn as an open
circle with its name, and names its net, the same on every page.
It may be said at several pins, and on several pages: each is drawn,
and they are one net.
After its name in brackets stand the numbers of every page that holds
it, its own too, in ascending order, separated by a comma and no space:
BUF(1,2); a port on one page alone has no list.
A port of the sheet on one page alone, in a file of several, is
warned of.
.B note
is for the reader ("keep this loop small"); the programs pass over
it.
Notes never name designators.
.SS Parts
A part statement starts with its kind, the name of a symbol (resistor,
capacitor, inductor, ferrite, diode, tvs, opamp, bnc, ...), and the
name of the part.
The value is the first other word: 499, 0.1\[mc]F, 14V, or the part
number of an integrated circuit, whose pin table the library holds.
.B rating
is a second value (0.5W),
.B mpn
the maker's part number,
.B side
the side for its text,
.B flip
trades the places of the pins of the shape's flip pair (the inputs of
an op amp), or mirrors a shape without one.
A pin is PART.PIN, as amp.out or rf.2, by name or by number.
.PP
.I WAY
.B from
.I PIN
places the part against the pin, its own pin facing back.
If WAY is the way the pin points, the part continues the line, past
the branches hung from the pin, with room for the junctions of the
net; if WAY is across, the part hangs from the pin's lead as a branch,
after the others.
.B beside
lays a part of the same shape in parallel, pin to pin: below one lying
across and right of one standing, or above and left if there is no
room, or the way said.
Then the part and those beside it stand as an even row about the line
the part was placed on, four units of the grid apart, the middle of the
row on the line, so of two neither stands on it; a short wire, a bar,
joins their pins of a kind at each end, and the line meets the bar in
its middle.
A row that would not be clear so stays as it was laid.
.B of
places a part next to another without joining them.
.B between
places a part of two pins along the line of the two pins, out the way
said past everything that stands across that span; its pin 1 joins the
first pin.
Each part takes the first place out along its way where it, its label
and its ground flags are clear of what is placed; labels go on the
first clear side.
A part placed in none of these ways stands to the right of all there
is.
.SS Wires and flags
.B wire
joins two pins without placing anything; the router finds the path,
on the side WAY of the two pins if it is said.
A wire closes a loop: the box round its two pins and the parts of its
net, with their labels, is kept free of the parts of other nets, so a
feedback loop stays small and an unrelated branch stands outside it.
.BR ground ,
.BR chassis ,
.B supply
and
.B global
put a flag on a pin, which joins it to the global net of the flag: GND,
CHASSIS, or the name of the supply or of the global.
Flags of one name join, whatever their kind and on whatever page.
A ground hangs under its pin; a supply, a global or a port stands at
the end of its line, past the branches.
A global is drawn as its name at the pin, by a small filled diamond of
its own, with no list of pages.
.B nc
marks a pin left unconnected on purpose: it is drawn with a small cross
at its end, and the netlist lists it as nc, not as a pin forgotten.
.SS Library
The symbols are looked up in the directory
.B symbols
next to the source, then in the libraries of the program
.RI ( schsym (5)).
The ferrites of the kind the page uses most are drawn without their
value, which goes without saying; every part shows its designator.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
page front
port IN amp.in
group amp
  note inverting, gain of 10
  opamp u LMH6609 flip
  resistor rin 1k left from u.in-
  resistor rf 10k between u.in- and u.out up
  ground at u.in+
  port in rin.2
end

page back
resistor load 50
port IN load.1
ground at load.2
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
schdraw(1), schnet(1), schsym(5), netlist(5)
